WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.320
Fieldnote one. Anatomy of a stack.

2
00:00:09.354 --> 00:00:12.124
What is actually running when
someone says “the stack”?

3
00:00:13.674 --> 00:00:15.804
AKS is one part of the stack.

4
00:00:16.114 --> 00:00:18.674
The stack is one development
and test environment,

5
00:00:18.674 --> 00:00:20.604
in one Azure resource group.

6
00:00:21.296 --> 00:00:23.226
A stack is a resource group:

7
00:00:23.296 --> 00:00:27.066
AKS, plus the Azure data
services around it.

8
00:00:27.376 --> 00:00:28.656
Not the cluster alone.

9
00:00:28.656 --> 00:00:33.866
spi up creates AKS and its
Azure resources together.

10
00:00:36.044 --> 00:00:38.614
Some of those resources
belong to a partition,

11
00:00:38.684 --> 00:00:40.134
and some are shared.

12
00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:43.564
For the example partition,
opendes, records,

13
00:00:43.564 --> 00:00:47.254
blobs, and events each have
their own Azure resource:

14
00:00:47.644 --> 00:00:52.614
a Cosmos DB account, a Storage
account, and a Service Bus namespace.

15
00:00:52.964 --> 00:00:57.254
Entitlements, identities, and Key
Vault are shared by the environment.

16
00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:03.604
Inside AKS, the OSDU services
run in one namespace,

17
00:01:03.604 --> 00:01:07.964
one image each. Every request reaches
them through the Istio gateway,

18
00:01:07.964 --> 00:01:11.134
which routes each API path to its service.

19
00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.750
Follow one request.

20
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:20.020
A client asks for the opendes partition,
with a bearer token and a data partition

21
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.300
id. The gateway routes it
to the Partition service,

22
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.990
which identifies the caller
and reads stored configuration:

23
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.990
tables in common Storage.

24
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.630
This lookup never touches
the partition's Cosmos DB,

25
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.790
blob Storage, or Service Bus.

26
00:01:37.768 --> 00:01:38.928
An easy mistake:

27
00:01:38.928 --> 00:01:41.418
the stack is just AKS.

28
00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:47.328
AKS runs the Kubernetes workloads, but
the same resource group holds Azure data

29
00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:49.498
services outside the cluster.

30
00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:52.778
To check, list the resources in the group.

31
00:01:54.604 --> 00:01:55.934
So now you can say:

32
00:01:56.004 --> 00:01:59.854
the stack is AKS plus Azure data services.

33
00:01:59.924 --> 00:02:03.374
Partition resources, and shared resources.

34
00:02:03.764 --> 00:02:07.934
And the services run in one
namespace, behind one gateway.

35
00:02:10.548 --> 00:02:14.718
Next: how is the stack created,
and when is it usable?

